{"title":"Race and economic well-being in the United States","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/929816417/0/marginalrevolution~Race-and-economic-wellbeing-in-the-United-States.html","date":1764183374000,"content":"<blockquote><p>We construct a measure of consumption-equivalent welfare for Black and White Americans, which incorporates life expectancy, consumption, leisure, and inequality. Based on these factors, welfare for Black Americans was 40 percent of that for White Americans in 1984 and 59 percent by 2022. There has been remarkable progress for Black Americans: The level of their consumption-equivalent welfare increased by a factor of 3.5 over the last 38 years when aggregate consumption per person only doubled. Despite this progress, the welfare gap in 2022 remains disconcertingly large at 41 percent, much larger than the 16 percent gap in consumption per person.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>That is from a new article by <span>Jean-Félix Brouillette, Charles I. Jones, and Peter J. Klenow, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aeri.20240467\" target=\"_blank\">just published</a> in <em>American Economic Journal: Insights</em>.</span></p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/race-and-economic-well-being-in-the-united-states.html\">Race and economic well-being in the United States</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/929816417/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/929816417/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/929816417/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https:/